1. If \( \lfloor x \rfloor = 1 \), then \( x \) is in the interval \( [1, 2) \) and \( N = x^1 = x \). So \( N \) can be any integer from 1 to 1 (since \( x < 2 \) and \( x \) must be an integer).
2. If \( \lfloor x \rfloor = 2 \), then \( x \) is in the interval \( [2, 3) \) and \( N = x^2 \). So \( N \) can be any integer from \( 2^2 = 4 \) to \( 3^2 - 1 = 8 \).
3. If \( \lfloor x \rfloor = 3 \), then \( x \) is in the interval \( [3, 4) \) and \( N = x^3 \). So \( N \) can be any integer from \( 3^3 = 27 \) to \( 4^3 - 1 = 63 \).
4. If \( \lfloor x \rfloor = 4 \), then \( x \) is in the interval \( [4, 5) \) and \( N = x^4 \). So \( N \) can be any integer from \( 4^4 = 256 \) to \( 5^4 - 1 = 624 \).
5. If \( \lfloor x \rfloor = 5 \), then \( x \) is in the interval \( [5, 6) \) and \( N = x^5 \). So \( N \) can be any integer from \( 5^5 = 3125 \) to \( 6^5 - 1 = 7775 \). However, since \( N \) must be less than 1000, the only possible value is \( 3125 - 1 = 7775 - 1 = 720 \).

So we need to count all the possible values of \( N \) from the above intervals that are less than 1000. We can use Python to automate this counting process.

